      <h1>How Pre-Foreclosure Works in Texas and What Your Options Are</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Falling behind on your mortgage doesn't mean you've run out of options. Understanding where you stand in the process is the first step toward making a clear-headed decision.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container article-layout">
      <article class="article-body">

        <div class="article-intro">
          <p>Pre-foreclosure is the period between when a homeowner falls behind on mortgage payments and when the lender actually takes the property. In Texas, that window can be shorter than most people expect. But it's also the window where homeowners have the most options. If you're in this situation, the worst thing you can do is wait and hope it resolves on its own. The best thing you can do is understand exactly where you are in the process and what moves are available to you right now.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>What Pre-Foreclosure Actually Means</h2>
        <p>Pre-foreclosure is not a formal legal status in Texas. It's a general term for the period after a homeowner has missed mortgage payments and the lender has begun the process that could lead to foreclosure, but before the property has actually been taken or sold at auction.</p>
        <p>Lenders typically consider a loan in default after 90 days of missed payments, though the exact terms depend on your loan agreement. Once that happens, the clock starts moving. In Texas, the foreclosure process can move quickly compared to many other states, which is why understanding your timeline matters.</p>

        <h2>How Texas Foreclosure Works</h2>
        <p>Texas is a non-judicial foreclosure state. That means lenders can foreclose without going to court, which is part of why the timeline here is compressed compared to states that require a judge to sign off on every step.</p>

        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 1</div>
            <h4>Missed payments and default notice</h4>
            <p>After you miss payments, your lender will send a notice of default. This is a formal communication that you've violated the terms of your loan. It's also often the first moment when homeowners realize they need to act. Some lenders are quicker than others to send this; many will attempt contact and offer workout options before formal notices go out.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 2</div>
            <h4>Notice of Sale</h4>
            <p>Under Texas law, the lender must send you a written notice of the intent to accelerate the loan at least 20 days before a Notice of Sale is filed. Once that notice period passes without resolution, the lender files the Notice of Sale. This notice must be mailed to you at least 21 days before the foreclosure sale date and posted publicly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Step 3</div>
            <h4>Foreclosure sale (first Tuesday of the month)</h4>
            <p>Texas foreclosure sales happen on the first Tuesday of each month at the county courthouse. If the property goes to auction and no one bids more than what's owed, the lender takes it back as REO (real estate owned). If a third-party buyer bids higher, they get the property. Either way, the homeowner is out at this point.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <strong>The key thing to know:</strong> From the first missed payment to the foreclosure sale, the timeline in Texas can be as short as a few months. Unlike some states where the process drags on for a year or more, Texas moves fast. The pre-foreclosure window is real, but it's not unlimited.
        </div>

        <h2>Your Options During Pre-Foreclosure</h2>
        <p>This is where most guides start offering false hope or generic advice. Here's a straightforward breakdown of what options actually exist and what each one requires.</p>

        <div class="qa-list">
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Reinstatement</h4>
            <p>If you can come up with the full amount of past-due payments, fees, and penalties, you can reinstate the loan and stop the foreclosure. Texas law gives you the right to reinstate the loan up to 20 days before the scheduled foreclosure sale. This is the cleanest outcome if you have the funds available, because you keep the property and the loan continues as before. The challenge is that by the time many people look into this, the reinstatement amount has grown significantly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Loan modification</h4>
            <p>A loan modification restructures the terms of your loan to make payments more manageable going forward. This might mean extending the loan term, reducing the interest rate, or rolling missed payments into the back end of the loan. Modifications require lender approval and take time. If you're going this route, start the conversation with your lender early. Waiting until a few weeks before the foreclosure sale makes approval much harder to get in time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Forbearance agreement</h4>
            <p>Some lenders will agree to temporarily reduce or pause your payments while you get back on your feet. Forbearance buys time but doesn't eliminate what you owe. The missed payments typically need to be made up through a repayment plan afterward. This option works best when the hardship is temporary and you have a clear path to getting current.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Selling the property</h4>
            <p>If you have equity in the home, selling before the foreclosure sale is often the most financially sound option. You pay off what's owed to the lender, recover any remaining equity, and walk away without a foreclosure on your record. The difference between a standard sale and a distressed sale is timeline. We work with homeowners who need to close quickly and can structure transactions that work within the pre-foreclosure window.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Short sale</h4>
            <p>If you owe more than the home is worth, a short sale allows you to sell the property for less than the outstanding loan balance with lender approval. The lender agrees to accept the proceeds as full satisfaction of the debt, or sometimes with a partial deficiency. Short sales require lender cooperation and take longer than a standard sale, which can be a problem given Texas's compressed foreclosure timeline. They're still worth exploring if you're underwater, because foreclosure is typically the worst outcome for your credit and your finances.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Deed in lieu of foreclosure</h4>
            <p>A deed in lieu means you voluntarily transfer the property to the lender in exchange for being released from the mortgage debt. It avoids a formal foreclosure, which is somewhat better for your credit, but you lose the property. Lenders don't always accept these, particularly if there are other liens or encumbrances on the property. It's an option worth asking your lender about if the other paths are closed.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="qa-item">
            <h4>Bankruptcy</h4>
            <p>Filing for bankruptcy triggers an automatic stay that immediately halts the foreclosure process. This can buy time, but it's not a permanent solution to a mortgage default. Chapter 13 bankruptcy allows you to restructure debts and create a repayment plan that can include bringing your mortgage current over time. This is a significant legal step with long-term financial consequences. If you're considering it, talk to a bankruptcy attorney, not a real estate agent.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>What Happens to Your Credit</h2>
        <p>A completed foreclosure is one of the most damaging things that can happen to your credit. It typically stays on your credit report for seven years and can drop your score by 100 points or more depending on your starting point. It also makes qualifying for a new mortgage significantly harder for years afterward.</p>
        <p>A short sale is generally less damaging than a foreclosure, though it still appears on your credit report. A deed in lieu is similar. Missing payments during the pre-foreclosure period also affects your credit, regardless of how the situation ultimately resolves.</p>
        <p>The point isn't to scare you. It's to make clear that the sooner you act, the more options you have to limit the damage.</p>

        <h2>What to Do Right Now</h2>
        <p>If you're behind on payments or have received any formal notices from your lender, here's the order of operations that makes sense for most situations:</p>

        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">First</div>
            <h4>Find out exactly where you stand</h4>
            <p>Call your lender's loss mitigation department and ask specifically where you are in their process, what the reinstatement amount is, and what workout options they offer. Get this in writing. Don't assume your servicer's customer service rep has the full picture.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Second</div>
            <h4>Get an honest read on your property's value</h4>
            <p>Knowing what your home is actually worth right now tells you which options are on the table. If you have equity, selling is a real option. If you're underwater, you're looking at different paths. This conversation costs you nothing and takes less than an hour.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Third</div>
            <h4>Decide on a path quickly</h4>
            <p>The worst thing you can do in pre-foreclosure is delay. Every week you wait is a week closer to the first Tuesday of the month. If you're going to sell, the sooner you start, the more time you have to close properly. If you're pursuing a modification, you need time for the lender to process it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Fourth</div>
            <h4>Work with people who understand the timeline</h4>
            <p>A traditional real estate listing can take 45 to 90 days or more from decision to close. In some pre-foreclosure situations, that's fine. In others, it's not fast enough. We work with homeowners who need more flexibility on timeline and can structure offers and closings that fit tighter windows.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>What We Do in These Situations</h2>
        <p>We work with homeowners across DFW who are dealing with situations that don't fit a standard listing. That includes pre-foreclosure. We can give you an honest read on what the property is worth, what your options look like, and whether a direct sale makes sense given your timeline. If a traditional listing is the better move, we'll tell you that too. We don't have an interest in pushing you toward any particular outcome.</p>
        <p>We're not a foreclosure rescue company and we don't make promises about stopping foreclosures. What we can do is help you understand your options clearly and move quickly if that's what the situation requires.</p>

